121 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 96 0 R
/E (The United Nations Economic and Social Council \(ECOSOC\) was established under Article 7 of the Charter of the United Nations \(1945\) as one of the six principal organs of the UN, tasked with coordinating the economic, social, and related work of the seventeen UN specialized agencies, ten functional commissions, and five regional commissions. ECOSOC serves as the central platform for fostering debate, reflection, and innovative thinking on sustainable development, convening the UN system, Member States, civil society, and the private sector around the achievement of the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development. Comprising 54 Member States elected by the General Assembly for staggered three-year terms, ECOSOC holds a annual cycle of meetings culminating in a four-week substantive session each July, alternating between New York and Geneva. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
122 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 96 0 R
/E (ECOSOC's mandate, most clearly outlined under Articles 62 through 66 of the UN Charter, empowers it to make or initiate studies and reports on international economic, social, cultural, educational, health, and related matters; to make recommendations to the General Assembly, UN Member States, and specialized agencies; to prepare draft conventions for submission to the General Assembly; to call international conferences; and to coordinate the activities of the specialized agencies through consultation with and recommendations to them. Council resolutions are non-binding, but carry significant normative and political weight, often shaping the work of subsidiary bodies and funds such as the United Nations Development Programme \(UNDP\), the World Food Programme \(WFP\), the United Nations Children's Fund \(UNICEF\), and the Office of the UN High Commissioner for Refugees \(UNHCR\). )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
