154 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (Reintegration illustrates both the opportunity and the fragility of post-conflict recovery. Following the fall of the Assad government, approximately 1.3 million refugees and nearly 1 million IDPs returned home during 2025. Yet Syria still required humanitarian assistance for over 15.6 million people, more than 60% of its population. Neighboring nations such as Jordan, Turkey and Uganda mount significant monetary and social strain from long term displacement. ECOSOC must operate as though reintegration is an essential pillar of long-term recovery rather than addressing it through isolated humanitarian channels. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
155 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (Education Continuity and Long-Term Human Development )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
156 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (Disruption to education directly affects the long-termeconomiccost of conflict by weakening the future workforce a post-conflict state will need to rebuild. UNICEF estimates that more than 52 million children in conflict-affected countries are out of school, while a further 234 million school-aged children living in crisis settings require urgent support simply to access quality education. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
157 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (In Gaza, 95.5 percent of all 815 schools were damaged or destroyed and all 637,000 school-aged children lacked access to formal education as of the end of 2025. In Ukraine, more than 1,600 educational facilities have been damaged or destroyed since 2022, leaving 4.6 million children facing educational barriers in the 2025-2026 school year. Across West and Central Africa, more than 14,000 schools remain closed due to conflict, affecting 2.8 million children. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
158 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (If left neglected, this disruption risks creating what UN officials have termed a 'lost generation', a workforce and civic base without the foundational education that post-conflict economies require to escape cycles of poverty and renewed instability. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
159 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (UNICEF's 2025 Annual Report indicates that severe funding cuts and supply shortages threaten critical medical care, including immunization, for more than 20 million children worldwide, with conflict zones bearing the heaviest impact. Rebuilding healthcare systems and infrastructure in post-conflict states requires a comprehensive approach that goes beyond physical reconstruction, re-establishing supply chains for medicines, retraining health workers who fled during conflict, and restoring routine services such as maternal care and immunization that are often the first casualties of prolonged instability. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
